  "visual": "memory",
  "visuals": [
    "branch",
    "memory"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "Often there are more than two choices. `elif` (short for \"else if\") adds another test:\n\n```\nif speed > 120:\n    print(\"Way too fast\")\nelif speed > 100:\n    print(\"Too fast\")\nelse:\n    print(\"OK\")\n```\n\nPython checks the tests **from the top** and runs the block of the **first** one that is True. After that it skips everything else in the chain, without even checking the other tests. `else` at the end catches anything no test caught.\n\nBecause the first True wins, **order matters**: put the strictest test first.",
    "analogy": "Sorting letters into slots on a wall from top to bottom. You hold a letter against the first slot; if it fits, it goes in and you are done. If not, you try the next slot down. The bottom slot (else) takes anything left."
  },
